{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-05-successive-traders-resale-chains",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Successive Traders and Resale Chains",
    "description": "Follow an item’s cost through successive sellers, applying each trader’s margin to that trader’s own cost.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Model successive sale stages using multiplicative price factors: each new seller's SP becomes the next seller's CP. Recover an original production cost from a final retail price, or solve a missing margin in a chain. Include stages with gains and losses, at least three traders, and distinguish a percentage on the current stage's CP from one on the original CP. Check by applying the factors forward. Use solved example 21 and objective questions about manufacturers, wholesalers, retailers, and defective goods."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Successive Traders and Resale Chains: Follow an item’s cost through successive sellers, applying each trader’s margin to that trader’s own cost. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "In a chain, one trader’s selling price becomes the next trader’s cost. Each gain or loss factor applies to the current stage’s cost unless wording explicitly supplies a different base.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose a producer spends ₹400 and earns 10%. The wholesaler then earns 20% on the ₹440 paid, and the retailer earns 25% on the ₹528 paid. The customer pays ₹660.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The forward model is multiplication: final price=initial cost×1.10×1.20×1.25. Multipliers are applied in sequence because each percentage is based on the previous stage’s amount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To recover the first cost from a final price, divide by the product of all factors. If the final price is ₹660 under those three margins, cost=660/(1.1×1.2×1.25)=₹400.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A stage can incur a loss. Use a retained factor such as 0.95 for a 5% loss, then continue from that stage’s SP as the next cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A trader’s percent margin is not automatically a percent of the original manufacturing cost. If the wholesaler adds 20% to the invoice received, use the invoice as the wholesaler’s CP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If total chain increase is given and all but one trader’s rate are known, solve the missing multiplier. Divide the overall factor by the product of known stages, then subtract 1.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For example, total price is 65% above production cost, while first two stages use factors 1.20 and 1.25. The last factor is 1.65/(1.20×1.25)=1.10, so the last trader earns 10%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Stage labels help: producer cost, wholesale SP/retail CP, retail SP/customer price. Writing each intermediate amount catches a repeated or skipped percentage.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a product becomes defective during transport, a later seller’s loss is still applied to the price that seller paid, not to the producer’s original cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Multiple consecutive equal gains compound. Three stages each gaining 10% multiply by 1.1³=1.331, a 33.1% overall increase, not 30%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A sequence with gains and losses may partially offset. A 20% gain followed by a 20% loss gives 1.2×0.8=0.96, so final amount is 4% below the starting amount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check the order: multiplying factors is commutative for a pure total factor, but the story of intermediate CPs still matters when computing an individual trader’s money profit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep currency and rate separate at every stage; then check that each SP equals the next CP and the final receipt matches the stated total.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Finish by naming the requested stage. A question can ask producer cost, retailer profit, total increase, or the missing trader rate; these are not interchangeable.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Name the cost, receipt, and percentage base. Choose an operation that follows the transaction wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
